hi there steve kaufman here  uh i promised you shorter videos with uh  captions and translations  but today is not one of them today is uh  christmas day so i'm going to ramble a  bit and talk about christmas  and  try to somehow connect it to language  learning  christmas  um  you know many of the traditions that we  identify with christmas  of course historians will tell you that  it's sort of an amalgamation of  um aspects of ancient middle eastern  religions  uh for example uh a virgin giving birth  to a child is a tradition that existed  in other religions in the middle east  some of the traditions relate to roman  festivals saturnalia  obviously  in an attempt to appeal to the roman  population  christmas trees  christmas lights candles on christmas  trees apparently  come from the tradition in northern  europe of having great bonfires on the  darkest day of the year in order to  encourage the sun to come back and so  forth and so on  so i mean everything in in this  wonderful rich  uh human sort of  cultural tapestry that we live today is  the accumulation of  what people did before us  whether it be technology i mean modern  technology wouldn't be anywhere without  the contribution of chinese technology  and arab  technology and hindu mathematics  long ago  i mean the food that we eat today  so typically italian like tomatoes or  typically irish or german like potatoes  and and all these things came from the  new world uh peppers and so forth and so  on so everything that we enjoy today is  is a part of this  um  increasing globalization fusion mixture  of traditions and and foods and so forth  now within that  i feel that the phenomenon of  christianity is is quite spectacular  it started in the middle east  and initially most of the adepts lived  in the middle east north africa sort of  around the uh  around the mediterranean basin  today  uh the number of christians in that part  of the world is relatively small  although they do there are some  christianity today represents about one  third of the population of the world  speaking  a variety of languages obviously very  heavy in the americas therefore speaking  english french in  northern in north america uh spanish in  mexico with zija which is also part of  north america and then spanish and  portuguese through the rest of the  americas a little bit of french in haiti  and so forth  english course in guyana  and then of course this tremendous  growth of christianity in africa where  we have a wide variety of languages  all spoken by christians  and now in uh in asia and the pacific  islands uh  christianity has been growing  so that uh christianity which was  initially a religion of the middle east  became a religion of europe thanks to uh  rome imperial rome  uh but now the europe europe per se is a  relatively smaller party in fact if  anything christianity is in decline in  europe as people are becoming more  secular on the other hand is growing in  other parts of the world  uh so  in that sense christianity unites a lot  of people around a common  religion common granted there are  catholics and protestants and orthodox  and a great variety of christians  however  the the basic message of  christ i'm not a christian i'm not a  religious person but the basic message  of christ and the message of  christmas  is a very positive one it's about  peace  christ's message was one of helping the  weak the less fortunate  being tolerant being forgiving  uh and and obviously this was a very  powerful message because what started  out as  a few thousand people are very small  sort of  sect  uh in the middle east  uh  through a variety of circumstances the  their message or even the demographics  which is very powerful apparently the  christians had  better demographics because they help  each other better during times of plague  and disease and so forth and today of  course  as was pointed out by my son  who is a professor at the university of  london birkbeck college and he wrote a  book called the religious shall inherit  the earth  people who are religious have larger  families and so while there might be a  decline in religiosity in certain  countries of europe as people become  more secular this is more than  compensated for  by the high  fertility birth rate of the more  religious people whether they be  christians muslims  jews for that matter and but the message  of christmas really is a message that's  quite common to all religions after all  uh while  christians might account for somewhat  over 2 billion people in the world  muslims account for  not that much less  and of course we have hindus and we have  buddhists and in all of these religions  people are expressing largely  the same  need for human solidarity for peace  unfortunately  like everything human  it comes with the good and the bad  and so there have been  many examples of intolerance within  religion  fighting between sets between religions  and all the things that we really can't  just ascribe to religion per se but  rather ascribe to the difficult nature  of human beings  who find all kinds of reasons to fight  and squabble and be nasty to each other  now how does all of that relate back to  my favorite topic language learning  it's a stretch  however the fact that  christianity because we are talking not  about all religions we're talking about  the christian religion today  is a religion practiced by  i don't know apparently two-thirds of  the countries in the world and so  therefore the wide range of the range of  languages spoken by christians is  enormous  so there's a connection  uh  i think to learn about religions is to  learn about human beings just as to  learn about languages is to learn about  human beings  so you know what the attitude that i  have and i must say that my family  we're not religious but we also  celebrate christmas as many  non-christians do because we give  presents and we have  a dinner one evening at our place with  our uh son and his family and the  following day we're invited over to  their place with  with my son's mother-in-law and  brother-in-law  and so that it becomes an opportunity to  get together  uh which uh  you know is a wonderful thing for  christians to do  but it's also something that people of  all religions enjoy doing  so i guess  christmas the message is supposed to be  one of peace  uh  it's also  much commercialized but you can't blame  christianity for that that's also in our  nature we tend to be acquisitive  but uh but we like to give gifts and we  like to buy things for people we like to  think about what make what make them  what might make them happy and plus  we're happy when others think of us so  there you go it's a bit of a ramble  it's not  anything to do with language learning  those messages are coming in the new  year they'll be shorter  and they will have transcripts and  captions but this one doesn't so i wish  you all a merry christmas those of you  who are christians or who celebrate  christmas and i wish everyone a very  happy end of the year because there are  other holidays during this time of year  i wish everyone  a happy uh holiday period  and i hope that when you contemplate the  year that's gone by in the world we live  in that you do so with optimism and  generosity because i try to  i think we should all try to be upbeat  and on that note  i say goodbye and i look forward to  seeing you in the new year  bye for now

